  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{4}\u2061x-{sec}^{2}\u2061x?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}\u2061x-{sec}^{2}\u2061x & ={sec}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot ({sec}^{2}\u2061x-1) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot {tan}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}\u2061x{tan}^{2}\u2061x & =(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x){tan}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}\u2061x-{sec}^{2}\u2061x=(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x){tan}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$",
